 ## Overview
-This method renders volumetric clouds by evaluating a procedural noise function directly within a GPU fragment shader during a direct volume ray casting pass, eliminating the need for pre-computed voxel textures or light maps [S1, unofficial]. It differs from texture-based approaches by computing density on-the-fly using Fractal Brownian Motion (FBM) of Worley noise, and from light-map techniques by approximating light transport via analytic Henyey-Greenstein scattering and simplified transmittance marches rather than storing pre-baked illumination data [S1, unofficial].
+This method renders volumetric clouds by evaluating density and lighting procedurally within a GPU fragment shader, replacing static 3D texture lookups with analytical noise functions and on-the-fly light transport calculations [S1, unofficial]. It differs from texture-based volume rendering by computing density $\rho(\mathbf{p})$ via Fractal Brownian Motion (FBM) of Worley noise and approximating light scattering using the Henyey-Greenstein phase function and Beer-Lambert transmittance, allowing for dynamic, infinite-resolution clouds without large memory footprints [S1, unofficial].
 
